<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="e0385ae1">&lt;p&gt;
    Understand how to identify, raise and remove any impediments that are preventing the team from making progress.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;
    Identify
&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    In the Agile Framework, impediments are not only issues but could encompass risks or any type of &quot;blocker&quot;.
    Essentially, impediments are considered anything that prevents, blocks or impedes the team from making progress. Risks
    can, and should be handled differently, for example the risk of:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Low product adoption is typically mitigated by early and frequent reviews with a subset of the eventual product
        users
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        How various parts of the product are designed is typically mitigated by working iteratively and incrementally
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Insufficient knowledge is mitigated by running experiments to confirm or dismiss assumptions, which is typically
        noted as a Backlog Item
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;
    Raise
&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Once an impediment has been identified, it is essential to raise it as soon as possible. This is ideally done during
    daily team meetings (e.g. daily Scrum meeting). Once the daily meeting concludes, a focused group of relevant team
    members can discuss the impediments. Depending on team agreements and severity of an impediment, it may be raised
    outside of scheduled meetings to expedite the removal process.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;
    Remove
&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Those directly interested in, or involved with, the impediments raised regroup immediately, &quot;swarming&quot; around the issue
    to swiftly resolve it. Separating the process for &quot;raise&quot; from &quot;remove&quot; prevents the broader group from being bogged
    down by issues that are not relevant to them. Additionally, it allows the appropriate group to focus their effort on
    determining how to remove the identified impediment.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    If the impediment is easily translated into an actionable task, it should be added to the Product, or Sprint, Backlog.
    This means items posing an immediate threat, requiring resolution during the current sprint, should be added to the
    Sprint Backlog. Meanwhile, an impediment may be raised that cannot, or should not, consume the team&apos;s time during the
    current sprint. If this is the case, the impediment should be added to the Product Backlog so it can be easily tracked,
    not forgotten and worked on as soon as is appropriate. With this approach, impediments are tracked and managed as work.
    They are not maintained separately but rather directly by the people responsible for removing the impediment.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;
    Conclusion
&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Overall, impediments should not be relegated to a constantly growing list of concerns to be addressed at some point in
    the future. Rather, impediments should be viewed as items requiring immediate attention and resolution so that they can
    be removed from blocking team progress.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
